      "status": "mathematical proof reconstructed",
      "correspondence": "Each determinant differentiation inserts (it)^n det(Gram X) in the Gaussian transform. The modified transform is integrable, so mollified Fourier inversion identifies its density by testing away from det B=0. Dividing out the determinant gives the value. In rank one two opposite signs give 1/[4(1+y²)] and the two squared radii give the same density directly. A constant after shifting to w=−1 has zero second derivative. At B=0 on the real axis the positive integral blows up when w approaches −1/2 from above.",
      "review": "An independent reader verified the normalization by polar coordinates, the rank-one Hermitian coordinate and the derivative at w=−1. Numerical oscillatory quadrature at |B|=1/4 and 1/2 and shifts w=0 and 1 agreed with both explicit Fourier formulas to 19 decimal places."
